now we've been talking a lot about the  new strain of covid on the programme  today matt hunk told us earlier that dr  susan hopkins  of public health england is probably the  leading expert in the country on this  new strain so it's very convenient that  we can now speak to her to find out more  thank you so much for being on the  program  this morning so when was this new strain  first discovered and what alerted you to  it  so new variants are found all of the  time and in particular in this strain  this was  identified initially from a patient in  september  but the whole genome sequencing was  returned in  october even then there was nothing to  particularly highlight that this was  something a major concern as variants  come and go  in late november we were trying to  understand why  kent and medway were not uh reducing  with the national restrictions  and performed details clinical  behavioral and epidemiological  investigations including whole genome  sequencing  and found that there was a cluster that  was rapidly spreading  across kent and medway that was this  variant  which we then called a variant of  interest and we tried to understand  where else it had spread  and could see it was also spreading into  london and  also essex we notified the government  last friday week  and did further work with academic  partners looking at modeling and  understanding the virus  compared to other variants that are  circulating of which there are many  uh and found that the this week that  they it all  with all the evidence stacking up that  this variant  was much more transmissible than the  other variants in circulation  uh and that made us hugely concerned and  notified government on friday  um you notified the government on friday  but surely the prime minister must have  known before that you must have been  keeping the government informed  so absolutely we were talking to the  government on a daily basis the results  of the  modeling on transmissibility  came to uh were all summarized and  presented on friday morning to the new  and emerging respiratory virus threats  advisory group  by a number of different groups around  the country doing modeling  and phylogenetic evidence of the virus  so  that was the first time that all of the  evidence came together  where the nerve tag group felt that they  could confidently say with  moderate confidence that this had higher  transmissibility than the other viruses  that were circulating i know of course  you're you know a scientist  not a politician but just listening to  the timeline the fact that you're you  know keeping  everyone informed on a daily basis why  was the prime minister still insisting  would be inhuman to cancel christmas on  wednesday  uh we did not have the modeling evidence  to show that the transmissibility  was increased or that the ore value is  increased on wednesday  okay so you're saying that about you  knew that it was spreading to london to  south east  and the east of england is there any  evidence that this variant has spread to  other parts of the country as well  so yes we have evidence that there's  there's  virus in other parts of the country but  in very small amounts  we've got two methods that we're able to  detect this so first of all whole genome  sequencing  whole genome sequencing takes two to  three weeks to return results so that we  can analyze them because it's a highly  complicated process  we know that from the whole genome  sequencing there is small amounts  in other parts but about one in 20 of  the confirmed  laboratory confirmed cases have whole  genome sequencing so when we detect it  we know there's definitely some there  uh unlikely to have some spread we then  look  at the data from the office of national  statistics coronavirus infection survey  where we monitor uh the types of genes  that are detected  and in this case this variant is not  detected by one of the  laboratory tests using the s gene and so  we're able to monitor that across the  whole country  and what we can see is that the amount  of the s gene that we can't detect has  remained stable in the rest of the  country  but has increased very dramatically in  from middle november in kent and midway  and from the  end of november beginning of december in  london and east of england  we're not seeing that anywhere else in  the country and part of the reasons that  we were  increasingly confident that it was more  transmissible is that areas that were  seeing  rapidly rising transmission were also  areas where  this variant was becoming the dominant  virus  and that's where we start putting all of  the evidence together  to confirm this we'll need laboratory  studies and those will take some weeks  um reassuring to hear that it's only  really increasing as you say  in these specific parts of the country  but where else have you  detected it so as i said it's been  detected  in many other parts of the country every  region  has cases but with very small numbers  it's also been detected in wales  in scotland we've not yet detected in  northern ireland but whole genome  sequencing has not been performed on  very many specimens  in other parts of the world the similar  strain has been in detection in denmark  australia and there are other variants  that look very similar to this  that have been detected in south africa  in the netherlands and other countries  we're not yet confirmed that those these  are the exact same as this variant  because this variant has a very clear  genetic makeup that we can follow  okay and the prime minister said  yesterday that this new variant is  believed to be  up to 70 more transmissible how  confident are you in that 70  number so um we have done  uh work with our modeling experts at  imperial to compare this variant to the  other variants that are circulating  and look and uh how we think this is  transmitting  and causing increased numbers of cases  uh the  figure of 70 is is it looks like a good  one  but we don't have very good um  confidence intervals around that so the  confidence intervals are very wide at  the moment  we're doing further work looking at the  difference with the sort of esteem  dropout which we discovered last weekend  was a marker for this variant and that  will give us an  increased amount of cases that we can  track and follow and give us better  estimates over the coming weeks so we  hope  to refine those estimates though i think  70  looks like a good number to land on at  the moment  as we refine those we will of course  tell you and the public  okay thank you for that and really good  to know a bit more detail about that  figure  um we spoke to you debbie schroeder just  before the interview with you  who was saying that it's really too  early to know whether or not the  new virus could cause increased  mortality or whether it may react to the  vaccine  in a different way is that right is it  too early to know  so of course we don't have the  laboratory experiments for the vaccine  yet  um but the vaccine should induce a broad  immune response  um and therefore this uh the mutations  that we  see on uh the spike protein in this  particular variant  don't suggest that that vaccine will not  respond yet and that of course is done  with  hypothetical studies with protein  experts who who reconstruct  what this part of the virus looks like  with these mutations  what we are clear about is that we have  further laboratory work to do we're  growing the virus at the moment in  collindale and portland and imperial and  with the  virus grown we'll be able to look at the  uh how  the virus is killed by both people  who've had the infection before  um and also people who've had the  vaccine and those will give us to finish  the answers  but those are lengthy studies that will  take a couple of more weeks before we're  able to definitively say the response  and how about the increase to the  potential of increased  mortality and we were told yesterday  there's no evidence to suggest that  people are more likely to get sick or  more likely to die if they get this  particular variant  but of course we know there's a lag  between people catching the virus and  getting ill going to hospital and sadly  dying so is it too early to know that  so i think we've got no signals um so  the first signals we would expect to see  is in the southeast where this  has definitely increased over four weeks  now so we're seeing the increases there  i think it is too early to say in london  because those increases have only  happened in the last couple of weeks  but what we know is that the more cases  that we have in the community of this  virus the more cases we're seeing in  hospital  but we're not seeing a disproportionate  number of people being admitted to  hospital  over the last two weeks um and we're not  seeing any increases  in mortality yet we do have further  studies underway where we're studying  this variant versus other variants and  trying to link that with hospital  data so we get better estimates and  we're also looking  at further studies to try and understand  that in the laboratory  but again these things will continue to  emerge over the coming weeks  um really interesting thank you so much  for talking us through all the research  you're doing it's certainly what you  know i and lots of our viewers are  fascinated by and really interested in  this morning so thank you

